From 71014c8af58d562be6b72537b51400dac94db46a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 21:16:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09281/11362] Cap refinement rounds at 1, and a cross-level
 carry that never fired

REFINEMENT ROUNDS 3 -> 1 (arc_llm_reinduction.py). Rounds past the first are
harmful, not merely useless: exp5760 measured mean heldout delta 0.0, exp5766
measured pooled -0.0598, and 0 of 83 cells improved. They fired on 39 of 39
stalls at ~319s each, about 58% of stall wall-clock, and all 8 partial round-0
engines collapsed to 0.0. Cause is REQ-ARC-WMTE-6091: the refine prompt never
contains the engine being repaired, so a later round is a blind re-induction
from at most 5 mismatches -- less evidence than round 0 had.
CARNOT_ARC_MAX_REFINEMENT_ROUNDS restores the old behaviour without a source
edit, because fixing the underlying defect is a live separate lever and whoever
tests it needs the escape hatch. At cap 1, refactor() is never called: round 1
IS the induction. That surprised two test files which had silently depended on
multi-round behaviour; both now derive from the constant or raise the cap
explicitly rather than asserting a hardcoded 3.

CEGIS TOOL LOOP (default OFF). Diagnosis first: zero of 454 engine source lines
reach the refine prompt, which is the 6091 defect stated as a number. Wiring is
present and tested but not enabled -- it is the fix for the cause above, and it
has not been measured against the capped baseline yet.

CROSS-LEVEL ENGINE CARRY (default OFF) -- and the A/B is a VACUOUS null, which
matters more than the lever. carry_fires is 0 in all 9 paired cells, so the
measured -0.6 actions out of ~1450 is RNG noise, not evidence that carrying an
engine buys nothing. The ON-arm attempt rows say why it never fired:
min_accuracy_bar is 1.0 on the new level, carried engines scored verify_accuracy
0.25 to 0.5, and every attempt logged carried_engine_failed_new_level_verification
after three deferrals for insufficient_new_level_evidence. So carried engines
genuinely do not transfer across a level boundary. Do NOT tune that 1.0 bar:
any defensible lower bar still rejects a 0.25-accuracy engine, and admitting one
would be worse than carrying nothing. Transfer is the blocker, not the bar.

A LARGER FINDING FROM THE SAME CAPTURES, recorded here because it bounds what
these levers can be worth. The induced goal predicate has never fired on a real
win state: 0 of 31 win states across 49 engine evaluations. Note that
goal_fired_on_win_states is a string like "0/1", so an int cast silently reads
0/0 -- parse it. Six evaluations show a nonzero goal false-positive rate up to
0.657, firing on non-wins while missing every win. Corroborated by n_planned 0
of 136 induction attempts and 33 of 33 constant on-disk goal predicates.
plan_in_model terminates only on is_level_complete, so a constant-False
predicate guarantees None at any budget. This is NOT the n_win_states=0 corpus
artifact: these captures hold 31 genuine level-ups, which is the check that
makes the null interpretable.
Bounded consequence: on the DEFAULT configuration, levels are won by exploration
stumbling into the win condition, not by planning, so engine-quality levers are
not levels levers. The qualifier is load-bearing -- CARNOT_ARC_STRUCTURED_NAV=1
(arc_competition_agent.py:7328, opt-in) routes nav games to a
correct-by-construction model and REQ-ARC-WMTE-5842 measured tu93 L0->L1 with
it. The 0 of 136 measures the LLM-induction path with that flag off.

STILL OWED, NOT FIXED HERE: the committed +0.054 for tool-assisted repair was
measured UNSEEDED while the shipped path seeds. This commit does not correct it.

142 tests pass across the touched modules. git status was checked before staging
and again after the test run; no tracked results/** file was rewritten.

